ws give us a sense of what the  timeline is here when did the big  domestic workers organizing push take  place and can you give us some sense of  what proportion of domestic workers are  now organized in the Union I actually  began my story with the Montgomery bus  boycott and I think that was an enormous  ly important moment for domestic workers  because the vast majority of women who  supported and participated in that  boycott were domestic workers and had it  not been for them the boycott would not  have been successful so I look at their  support for the boycott but I also look  at their leadership in the boycott so  there was a woman named Georgia Gilmore  who was a domestic worker who went to  the first mass meeting that was called  in Montgomery and decided that she  wanted to actually do something to  organize other women and she decided to  cook food she began to bake pies and  make chicken dinners and to sell these  as a way to raise money for the boycott  and she organized other domestic workers  to do the same thing and they ended up  throughout the course of the year  raising an enormous amount of money to  support the boycott and then we see a  number of local domestic worker groups  emerging around the country in Cleveland  Ohio in Detroit in Atlanta in New York  these various local groups come together  in 1971 and form a national organization  and they have a membership of 25,000  which is you know a pretty large number  SDS at its peak I think was around  30,000 so it's a very large number of  women around the country who have come  together and their biggest victory was  the passage of the Fair Labor Standards  Act in 1974 but put that in context  Pamela so 25,000 domestic workers are  now organized out of a total you'd  estimate of how many domestic workers in  the country as a whole maybe a million  and a half so it's a very small minority  of women who are organized but I'll just  say that I think social change always  happens with a very sma