e  National Alliance is powered by 48  affiliate organizations and the first  local chapter in Atlanta of over 20,000  housekeepers and caregivers in so thank  you so much and imma turn over to our  speakers thank you I want to start by  saying what an enormous pleasure it is  to be here and to be celebrating this  book with Pramila  it's a book that crosses so many of the  fields that I've written about and I'm  interested in that I'm completely amazed  to see how it moves not just one field  forward but several fields forward the  field of labor history which is where I  started out the field of women's history  the study of working women it moves all  of those into what you might call a  cutting edge place so Pramila I want to  start out by asking you how did you come  to write a book on domestic workers  after all we all know that domestic  workers are at the edge of our  intellectual consciousness not at the  center of it and yet you've managed I  don't need to put them at the center of  it but to think about how that Center  affects all of American history this  book I guess is very deeply personal to  me on some levels but also grew out of  my scholarly research my grandmother  worked as a domestic worker in South  Africa for most of her life and my  mother worked as a domestic worker for a  short period in South Africa as well and  my mother had a number of working-class  jobs and she really instilled in me a  value of work whatever kind of work  people did to value that work and I  think I took that lesson away with me  but in addition to that about 10 years  ago or so I started to read about and  observe domestic worker organizing here  in New York City by an organization  call domestic workers United which was  an amazing group of mostly immigrant  women from all around the world who  began to raise awareness about the  plight of domestic workers about the  abuse and the exploitation and I was  enormous ly inspired by their ability to  speak out and to challenge this  occupation that plays such a central  role in their lives and then of course  there's the question of the academic  imperative I there has not been that  much research in writing on domestic  work we have a lot of research and  writing about women's labor history and  of course your foundational to defining  that field of women's labor history and  there have been some important  monographs about domestic work and  domestic worker organizing but if you  look at the importance of this  occupation in women's history for both  workers and employers we just don't have  enough information especially told from  the perspective of workers so that was  my interest in writing the book  mentioned that there is not a lot of  work done on domestic workers and I  agree but partly that's because we've  always said there really weren't written  sources they weren't good sources so we  couldn't write about domestic workers  how did you get through that barrier the  archive is a huge problem when you are  writing about poor and working-class  women who tend not to leave the same  kinds of documents as other people who  tend not to do as much public writing as  other activists do so I began to think  about the ways in which these women  built their movement and crafted their  movement and the kinds of stories that  they told in particular about their  organizing and those stories really  became the center of my book because it  was in their words their understanding  of this occupation of the history of  this occupation it was their way of  bringing other women into the movement  and their way of thinking about how to  reform the occupation it tell us more  about that what do you mean by  storytelling what kinds of stories did  they tell and who told them well they  well they told stories about their labor  and they told stories about the ways  they were higher  about the kinds of treatment that they  got from their employers and they told  stories about history one women one one  woman who I write about his name  Geraldine Miller who lived in the Bronx  she was an organizer and a domestic  worker here in New York City she lived  in the Bronx  and she told stories about something  called a Bronx slave market and these  were street corners during the Great  Depression where african-american women  stood while they were waiting to be  hired as day workers and Miller told a  story about women who were waiting on  the street corners and employers would  come up and look for the women with the  most scarred knees and she told a story  because women would look for the most  scarred knees because those were the  women who scrubbed floors down on their  hands and knees and Miller told a story  as a way to I think highlight the  exploitation and the occupation but it  also became a platform for reform so as  she told his story she told the the  women we are never ever again going to  scrub floors down on all fours it's a  fascinating story because of course we  associate what sounds like a shape-up  you know we call it a slave market when  it has to do with women but the shape-up  was a common way of hiring unskilled men  to do manual labor  and now you've uncovered a moment where  women go through exactly the same ordeal  so what looked like a gender difference  for a lot of years turns out not to be  one absolutely and the term slave market  was actually given to those street  corners by Ella Baker and Marvell cook  who were two african-american women  organizers here in New York City in the  1930s who wrote an expose about the  treatment of domestic workers during the  Depression and it's interesting because  that names stuck and it's stuck for  women organizers in the 1960s who  probably had never read anything about  the slave markets of the 30s but it  certainly heard those stories and those  stories  became part of their organizing toolbox  so how does it happen and this is one of  the things that really it puzzles me  still even after reading your book you  know I've written a lot about organizing  working women and you know where I come  from the capacity to organize often in  here is in the skill of the worker in  the community that the worker creates  but we always imagine domestic work as  an isolated kind of job so how do you  organize domestic workers could you talk  a little bit about how the domestic  workers overcame that what looks like  relative isolation it's a very hard  occupation to organize and as you know  most labor leaders it during most of the  20th century had deemed domestic work on  organiza Boleyn the women who I write  about actually use public spaces as  sites of organizing so and it's true of  domestic worker organizing today so the  public parks the public buses become  sites of organizing the laundry rooms in  in apartment buildings there was one  woman in Atlanta Dorothea Bolden who  rode every bus in the city of Atlanta  handing out leaflets and talking to  women as she rode those buses and  encouraged them to come out to a meeting  so it's an interesting way to think  about how labor organizing is  transformed when you're looking at an  occupation like domestic work it moves  away from the workplace and into the  public sphere it is completely amazing  especially when you consider that most  domestic workers were excluded from that  vast panoply of legislation that began  in the 1930s so even earlier they  weren't protected by protective labor  legislation they weren't included in the  Social Security Act of 1935 they were  excluded from the Fair Labor Standards  Act  how did it happen that they actually  managed to get themselves covered by  these various acts yeah the the  exclusion is interesting because I think  it it sort of reveals the ways in which  domestic work was considered different  from other kinds of work partly because  it was work that took place in the  privacy of the home partly because the  workforce was largely african-american  and so it becomes a different kind of  labor a kind of labor that's not  recognized and that's not valued the  same way that other kinds of work is  valued and women organize they organize  to fight for those rights and those  protections the the women in this book  lobbied for and eventually won in 1974  minimum wage protections for domestic  workers for the first time nearly 50  years after the the New Deal legislation  was passed and that was an enormous  victory they testified before Congress  and story telling actually became a part  of that of their of their efforts as  well so going before Congress and  talking to feminist leaders at the time  about the kinds of work that they did  became a part of how they made that  claim for minimum wage so wasn't there a  conflict between the feminist leaders  who presumably were hiring domestic  servants and the domestic servants who  were trying to get rights which would be  paid for by the feminist leaders there  was definitely a conflict because these  feminist leaders were mostly  middle-class and were women who relied  on and employed domestic workers and in  some ways it was in their interest to  pay less and to extract more work out of  their employees and so there was a  conflict there but there was also an  important sector of the feminist  movement that was very supportive of  domestic worker organizing and I think  you Alice were actually a part of that  feminist movement because I believe you  were at Sarah Lawrence College in the  1970s  and Sarah Lawrence had a very important  contingent of feminist activists who  actually worked to build bridges with  domestic worker organizers and Carolyn  Reid who's one of the women I profile in  the book went to several meetings and  Institute's at Sarah Lawrence and so I  think it's important to think about the  feminist community is not a single  community but there were very important  people in the feminist community who  were supportive of and I think worked on  behalf of dom